  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current solution uses a random initial point and sequential optimization with varying beta values. To improve, we focus on:\n   - Coarser-to-finer optimization across multiple resolutions (140, 280, 560 points)\n   - Adaptive optimization parameters that scale with resolution\n   - Linear interpolation for parent solution upgrade\n   - Gradient-free stochastic optimization with large neighborhood moves\n   - Dynamic max_sweeps reduction for larger grids to balance exploration\nThe core limitation to address is local optima trapping, so we implement:\n   - Simulated tempering with beta scaling\n   - Large neighborhood local search combining point and mass moves\n   - Resolution boosting from parent grid\nThe approach leverages the fact that finer grids can achieve lower correlations through more frequent transitions, while optimizing parameters to balance exploration vs. compute.\n\n2. Mathematical specifications\n- Representation: Discretized [0,2] interval with n samples (140,280,560)\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and \u222bh dx = n/2\n- Objective: min(max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n- Constraints: Integral normalization and spatial domain boundaries\n- Optimization: Minimize obj(h) = -log(\u2211exp(-\u03b2|m - mref|)) + |corr_max - mref|(1+\u03b2) under \u2211h=n/2\n- State evolution: \n  - Feasibility repair via normalized interpolation\n  - Tempering schedule: \u03b2_k \u2190 \u03b2_max * (1 - k/iter_max)^(1/iterations)\n  - Local search: Exploit large neighborhood moves including:\n    * Single point perturbations\n    * Mass conservation violations followed by repair\n    * Linear subgradient method in neighborhoods\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n**Input:** Seed (default 42)\n**Stage 1:** Initialize\n- Start with initial_h_values if available (n=140)\n- Compute parametrization length L = n (140,280,560)\n- Set base_beta = 5.0 and max_beta = 100.0\n\n**Stage 2:** Global search (all n)\n- Simulated tempering loop for \u03b2 in [base_beta * 2^k for k in range(-2,3)] if time > 100s\n- Kernel: Minimize obj(h) subject to ||h||=1 using CG for finite dimension\n- Conditioned on remaining time: Reduce iterations as \u221d 1/sqrt(n)\n- Repair with: h_i = (h_i * L) / \u2211h_i if sum deviates > 1% tolerance\n\n**Stage 3:** Local search (for each n)\n- Set n_large = 140,280,560 with max_sweeps:\n  140: max_sweeps=1000, 280:400, 560:200\n- Neighborhood moves:\n  for i,j nearby:\n    h = move_point(h,i,j,rand_factor=0.1)\n    h = balance_integral(h, tolerance=1e-4)\n- Apply 5 perturbation strategies:\n  1. Large point jumps\n  2. Mass redistribution\n  3. Temporal consistency moves\n  4. Step-boundary jumps\n  5. Random gradient descent steps\n\n**Stage 4:** Validation\n- Project solution to satisfy: 0\u2264h_i\u22641, sum(h)=L/2\n- Compute final C by: np.max(np.correlate(project(h),project(1-h),'full'))*dx\n\n4. Search choices\n- Resolution prioritization: [140,280,560] as primary grids\n- Context-adaptive annealing: \u03b2 updates linear in samples\n- Dynamic step size: \u03b4 = max(0.1, 2*(n-140)/560)\n- Termination if: no improvement in 50 iterations OR time < 80%\n- Repair fallback: if |sum(h)-L/2| > 0.5, vectorize by \u03b4-normalization\n\n5. Budget allocation\n- Total budget: 1100s\n  * Initialization: 100s\n  * Global search (140): 300s\n  * Global search (280): 150s\n  * Global search (560): 100s\n  * Local search: 450s\n  * Validation & projection: 50s\n- Critical path: Local search with scaling\n\n6. Validation contract\n- Check: \n  1. sum(h) == len(h)/2 (within 1e-5)\n  2. np.isfinite(obj) and compute_c5(h) defined\n  3. 0.0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.0 for all i\n- Fallback: Return project(best_h, sum_constraint) if invalid\n- Metric: Return_c = compute_c5(h) * (2.0/len(h))\n- Implementation:\n  use np.clip with tolerance propagation\n  use self.integral_project for repair\n  use logging with barrier checks at 500s, 700s",
